Now for the design.

We need to design an experiment that answers: "How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and the choice of unit representations to InfLLM's performance across long-context tasks?"

Two independent dimensions:
- Dimension A: dynamic context memory lookup
  - Variable levels: OFF (no dynamic lookup; e.g., use full KV / naive sliding window / static chunking with fixed attention to all or recent) vs ON (InfLLM-style dynamic lookup: query-aware retrieval of top-K units at generation time).
  - Implementation sub-options: 
    - (i) no memory units — naive attention over all tokens (OOM at long context) or sliding-window baseline;
    - (ii) static/global memory: all chunks always attendable with uniform attention (no lookup) — this isolates the lookup from the chunking;
    - (iii) InfLLM lookup: per token, compute relevance between current token's hidden state and unit representations, select top-K by similarity, attend to those blocks' raw KV.
  - Also top-K as a knob: K=1,2,4,...; and lookup temperature/score function (dot product, cosine).
- Dimension B: unit representations (granularity/representation form)
  - Granularity levels: token-level, sentence-level, paragraph-level, fixed-size chunk (e.g., 128/256 tokens). Actually token-level as unit would just be the standard attention (each token its own unit) — that's the trivial case. Sentence/paragraph/chunk are coarser.
  - Representation form: mean-pooled hidden state vs. last token hidden state vs. first token; also whether to use separate "extended" representations vs the actual stored KV.
  - In InfLLM, unit representations are used (a) to compute lookup scores, and (b) optionally as representative vectors cached separately. The design should treat "unit representation construction" as the manipulated variable, while everything else fixed.

